WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.535 --> 00:00:06.437
In 2014,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.373
KPMG's leadership had a problem.

00:00:09.774 --> 00:00:11.308
Accounting

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:12.510
is boring.

00:00:13.778 --> 00:00:16.147
Apologies to any accountants in the room,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:18.649
but that really was the issue.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.054
The senior leadership of one
of the world's premier accounting firms

00:00:23.087 --> 00:00:26.957
had been working for a while
to improve morale and engagement

00:00:26.991 --> 00:00:30.628
across the nearly 30,000
employees of the firm.

00:00:30.661 --> 00:00:34.465
When they started,
morale was in the tank.

00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:38.069
Only about half of employees
had a favorable opinion of the firm

00:00:38.069 --> 00:00:39.336
when surveyed,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.472
which is to say about half of employees

00:00:41.505 --> 00:00:46.177
had an unfavorable opinion of the place
they continued to work.

00:00:47.244 --> 00:00:49.480
They had tried to pull
the standard levers,

00:00:49.513 --> 00:00:51.182
perks, pay increases,

00:00:51.215 --> 00:00:54.385
more flexibility, more
opportunities to advance.

00:00:54.418 --> 00:00:57.154
But their initial gains had leveled off.

00:00:57.755 --> 00:00:59.857
And it's easy to understand why.

00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:02.226
Accounting, in particular auditing,

00:01:02.259 --> 00:01:05.529
can be a boring and thankless job.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.066
For most of the day, you're staring
at documents and spreadsheets,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.368
you're sitting in a cubicle
provided by a client

00:01:11.402 --> 00:01:13.871
who doesn't actually want you there

00:01:13.871 --> 00:01:16.407
and doesn't want to answer
any more questions either.

00:01:17.675 --> 00:01:19.777
And so having run out
of traditional ideas,

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:22.713
KPMG's leaders decided
to do something different.

00:01:23.214 --> 00:01:27.551
They decided to put purpose
at the core of their engagement effort.

00:01:28.185 --> 00:01:30.955
And what they did first
was particularly bold.

00:01:31.555 --> 00:01:32.990
They told stories.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.360
They launched what they called
the We Shape History campaign,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.096
a promotional campaign
designed to tell the story

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:43.801
of how KPMG had been involved
in pivotal moments in world history.

00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:48.005
They told the story of President
Roosevelt’s Lend-Lease Act,

00:01:48.005 --> 00:01:51.408
which sent billions of dollars in aid
to the Allies during World War II,

00:01:51.442 --> 00:01:54.545
and how he tapped KPMG
to manage logistics.

00:01:55.179 --> 00:01:56.347
They told the story

00:01:56.347 --> 00:01:59.717
of how KPMG accountants
resolved conflicting financial claims,

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.719
which laid the groundwork for the release

00:02:01.752 --> 00:02:04.488
of 52 US hostages in Iran in 1981.

00:02:04.855 --> 00:02:08.792
They told the story of how KPMG
certified the election of Nelson Mandela

00:02:08.826 --> 00:02:10.594
in South Africa in 1994.

00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:15.199
They told stories about how KPMG's
work served a higher purpose

00:02:15.232 --> 00:02:18.502
and they hung posters everywhere
to remind everyone of those stories.

00:02:18.536 --> 00:02:21.405
It was bold and it worked.

00:02:21.772 --> 00:02:23.007
Sort of.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.142
It moved the needle a little bit.

00:02:25.176 --> 00:02:27.878
What they did next was brilliant.

00:02:28.512 --> 00:02:31.348
To understand why their next move
was so brilliant, though,

00:02:31.348 --> 00:02:34.051
we need to talk about purpose.

00:02:34.585 --> 00:02:37.588
See, most of us think
of an organization's purpose or mission

00:02:37.621 --> 00:02:39.957
as a bold and lofty ambition,

00:02:39.957 --> 00:02:43.427
like, helping win a world war
or certify a historic election.

00:02:43.994 --> 00:02:48.299
And most leaders think that to convey
a purpose that truly inspires,

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:51.902
they need a compelling answer
to the question, “why?”

00:02:51.936 --> 00:02:54.405
As in, why do we do what we do?

00:02:55.139 --> 00:02:56.674
And this is where it gets weird

00:02:56.674 --> 00:02:59.810
because then most leaders look
to their mission statement.

00:02:59.843 --> 00:03:02.179
And even though mission
is different than purpose,

00:03:02.213 --> 00:03:04.081
but that's a totally different talk.

00:03:04.815 --> 00:03:06.617
They look at their mission statement,

00:03:06.617 --> 00:03:09.019
they work to rewrite it
to make it more compelling.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:12.289
They go through rounds and rounds
of editing and focus-group testing,

00:03:12.323 --> 00:03:16.393
and when their heavily workshopped,
perfectly worded statement is complete,

00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:19.930
they send it out to employees
in emails that get deleted.

00:03:19.964 --> 00:03:22.633
They print it on posters that get ignored.

00:03:22.666 --> 00:03:26.070
They put it on a page
on the company website

00:03:26.103 --> 00:03:27.605
that no one visits.

00:03:27.972 --> 00:03:29.139
Because it turns out,

00:03:29.173 --> 00:03:32.876
most people are less inspired
by a compelling answer to “why”

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:38.115
and more motivated by a clear answer
to the question, “who?”

00:03:38.115 --> 00:03:41.885
As in, who is served
by the work that we do?

00:03:42.753 --> 00:03:44.388
I mean, think about yourself.

00:03:44.388 --> 00:03:47.424
If I asked you to think of a time
when you felt highly engaged

00:03:47.458 --> 00:03:48.726
and inspired at work,

00:03:48.759 --> 00:03:50.194
you probably wouldn't mention

00:03:50.194 --> 00:03:53.731
the time your boss recited the company
mission statement verbatim.

00:03:54.465 --> 00:03:57.768
Instead, you'd probably think
of the last time you got a "thank you"

00:03:57.801 --> 00:03:59.203
from a client or a coworker,

00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:03.440
the last time you felt your work
was important to someone else.

00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:06.710
To explain this further,
let's switch cubicles.

00:04:06.744 --> 00:04:09.647
Let's move from the cubicles
of auditors at an accounting firm

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.817
to the cubicles of student workers
at a university donation call center.

00:04:14.451 --> 00:04:16.287
You thought accounting was boring.

00:04:16.787 --> 00:04:19.523
Maybe you got called by one
of these student workers

00:04:19.556 --> 00:04:20.958
in one of these call centers.

00:04:20.991 --> 00:04:22.826
They call in the evenings.

00:04:22.826 --> 00:04:25.496
They always have
a perfectly worded script.

00:04:25.529 --> 00:04:28.165
It always ends in a request
for a donation.

00:04:28.165 --> 00:04:29.533
So you end up having to say,

00:04:29.533 --> 00:04:32.469
"No, I don't want to donate
1,000 dollars to the new stadium."

00:04:32.503 --> 00:04:35.973
"No, I don't want to donate
500 dollars to the new student union."

00:04:36.006 --> 00:04:38.642
"No, I don't want to donate
20 dollars and five cents

00:04:38.676 --> 00:04:41.011
to commemorate my graduation year."

00:04:41.345 --> 00:04:44.114
It's like some collegiate version
of "Green Eggs and Ham."

00:04:44.148 --> 00:04:47.151
"No, I don't want to donate
in a box or with a fox."

00:04:47.151 --> 00:04:49.653
"No, I don't want to donate
in a house with a mouse."

00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:51.755
"Kid, I don't want
to donate here or there,"

00:04:51.789 --> 00:04:54.325
"Kid, I'm just trying to pay
off my student loans ..."

00:04:54.358 --> 00:04:55.492
(Laughter)

00:04:56.093 --> 00:04:58.362
"And then you can call me back
about donating."

00:04:59.496 --> 00:05:02.066
Think about the person
on the other end of that line.

00:05:02.533 --> 00:05:04.301
They're sitting in a windowless room,

00:05:04.335 --> 00:05:07.304
they're constantly dialing people
destined to hang up on them,

00:05:07.304 --> 00:05:09.506
yell at them, or worse.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.108
It's got to be boring.

00:05:11.141 --> 00:05:13.043
It's got to be thankless.

00:05:13.077 --> 00:05:15.846
It's got to be draining.

00:05:16.413 --> 00:05:18.048
And you can see it in the numbers.

00:05:18.082 --> 00:05:22.419
Annual turnover in these types
of call center jobs exceeds 400 percent.

00:05:22.453 --> 00:05:25.289
You do the math on that,
that means that in any given year,

00:05:25.322 --> 00:05:28.292
the entire staff quits every three months.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:33.063
In fact, when Adam Grant and a team
of researchers were looking for ways

00:05:33.063 --> 00:05:35.699
to improve morale at a call center
at their university,

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:39.269
one of the first things they noticed
was a sign in one student's cubicle.

00:05:39.303 --> 00:05:40.437
It read,

00:05:40.471 --> 00:05:41.638
"Doing a good job here

00:05:41.638 --> 00:05:44.174
is like wetting your pants
in a dark suit."

00:05:44.208 --> 00:05:47.544
(Laughter)

00:05:48.278 --> 00:05:50.013
"You get a warm feeling ..."

00:05:50.047 --> 00:05:52.216
(Laughter)

00:05:52.249 --> 00:05:53.851
"but no one else notices."

00:05:53.884 --> 00:05:55.953
(Laughter)

00:05:57.554 --> 00:06:00.391
The researchers wanted
them to feel noticed,

00:06:00.424 --> 00:06:02.393
obviously not for wetting themselves.

00:06:03.026 --> 00:06:05.929
They wondered if they could get
the student workers to notice

00:06:05.929 --> 00:06:07.698
the difference they were making

00:06:07.698 --> 00:06:10.000
and if that would have
a positive effect on them.

00:06:10.868 --> 00:06:13.470
So they took the break time
that these workers received

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.305
and they used it to run an experiment.

00:06:15.339 --> 00:06:17.508
Some of the workers,
during one of the breaks,

00:06:17.541 --> 00:06:18.976
got to meet with a student

00:06:19.009 --> 00:06:23.414
who had received scholarship funds
raised by that call center.

00:06:23.447 --> 00:06:25.649
And they got to hear
how receiving those funds

00:06:25.649 --> 00:06:27.351
had made a positive impact on them.

00:06:27.384 --> 00:06:30.921
They got to meet their answer
to the question

00:06:30.954 --> 00:06:33.957
"who is served by the work that we do?"

00:06:34.825 --> 00:06:37.227
And when the researchers
followed up a month later,

00:06:37.261 --> 00:06:41.031
that little meeting had a big
impact on the workers.

00:06:41.064 --> 00:06:43.801
The workers who got to meet someone
who directly benefited

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:45.269
from the work they were doing,

00:06:45.302 --> 00:06:46.703
they worked twice as hard.

00:06:46.703 --> 00:06:48.872
They made double the number
of calls per hour,

00:06:48.872 --> 00:06:51.408
they spent double the number
of minutes on the phone.

00:06:51.408 --> 00:06:54.044
Their weekly revenue went from
an average of 400 dollars

00:06:54.077 --> 00:06:56.146
to more than 2,000 dollars in donations.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.451
I mean, it's impossible to overstate
how big this effect is.

00:07:00.818 --> 00:07:03.821
These workers, they didn't get
any additional perks or benefits.

00:07:03.821 --> 00:07:05.756
They didn't get any additional training.

00:07:05.756 --> 00:07:08.525
They certainly didn't get asked
to memorize and internalize

00:07:08.559 --> 00:07:10.360
the university's mission statement.

00:07:10.394 --> 00:07:12.763
Instead, they got a five-minute chat

00:07:12.796 --> 00:07:16.099
with someone whose life was made
better by the work they were doing.

00:07:16.467 --> 00:07:19.069
The researchers argued
that these workers were inspired

00:07:19.102 --> 00:07:21.939
by a sense of pro-social motivation,

00:07:21.972 --> 00:07:25.943
the desire to protect and promote
the well-being of others.

00:07:26.276 --> 00:07:28.579
And that word, pro-social,

00:07:28.612 --> 00:07:31.482
that points to what's wrong
with most organizations' attempts

00:07:31.515 --> 00:07:33.217
at talking purpose.

00:07:33.217 --> 00:07:37.020
When you're talking about growth
or shareholder value or disruption

00:07:37.054 --> 00:07:38.455
or even sustainability,

00:07:38.489 --> 00:07:41.792
it becomes awfully hard
to tell specific stories

00:07:41.825 --> 00:07:44.495
about specific people
whose life is made better

00:07:44.528 --> 00:07:46.530
by the work that you're doing.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:49.633
But pro-social purpose
is what we want from our work.

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:54.371
And it's what powered the second step
of KPMG's purpose initiative.

00:07:55.372 --> 00:07:57.007
After the promotional campaign,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.345
after the corporate propaganda
of the We Shape History campaign,

00:08:01.378 --> 00:08:05.015
the leaders launched what they called
the 10,000 Stories Challenge.

00:08:05.382 --> 00:08:07.217
In essence, they said to their people,

00:08:07.217 --> 00:08:09.753
"You've heard how we've made
a difference in the past.

00:08:09.786 --> 00:08:12.789
Now you tell us how you're making
a difference right now."

00:08:12.823 --> 00:08:16.827
They set up an online application
that not only captured individual answers,

00:08:16.827 --> 00:08:19.696
but let people design
their own version of the poster

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.065
like the one from
the We Shaped History campaign.

00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:24.701
And the answers started rolling in.

00:08:24.735 --> 00:08:26.069
Answers like,

00:08:26.103 --> 00:08:28.038
"I combat terrorism

00:08:28.038 --> 00:08:30.173
because I help banks
prevent money laundering

00:08:30.207 --> 00:08:31.975
that can go toward terrorist groups."

00:08:31.975 --> 00:08:34.645
Or, “I help farmers grow

00:08:34.678 --> 00:08:38.348
because I support the farm credit system
that keeps family farms in business."

00:08:38.382 --> 00:08:40.784
Or, “I restore neighborhoods

00:08:40.817 --> 00:08:42.986
because I audit
community development programs

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:45.422
that revitalize low-income communities."

00:08:45.455 --> 00:08:47.491
They wanted 10,000 stories.

00:08:47.791 --> 00:08:52.362
They got 42,000 stories.

00:08:53.196 --> 00:08:55.832
And in time they also got
massive increases in morale

00:08:55.866 --> 00:08:58.168
and engagement across the whole company.

00:08:58.702 --> 00:09:02.039
But it's important to emphasize why
they got such a profound response.

00:09:02.072 --> 00:09:03.540
They got 42,000 stories

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.544
because they stopped talking
about purpose as a collective why,

00:09:07.578 --> 00:09:10.647
and started talking about it
as an individual who.

00:09:10.681 --> 00:09:14.084
They didn’t give every employee
a uniform answer to the question,

00:09:14.117 --> 00:09:15.452
“Why do we do what we do?”

00:09:15.485 --> 00:09:20.190
They helped each employee find
a specific answer to the question,

00:09:20.223 --> 00:09:23.126
“Who is served by the work that we do?”

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.796
And just like in the call-center research,

00:09:25.829 --> 00:09:28.799
that switch made
all the difference in the world.

00:09:29.933 --> 00:09:31.468
So what does this mean for you?

00:09:32.135 --> 00:09:33.904
Well, if you're in a leadership role,

00:09:33.937 --> 00:09:39.042
it means part of your job
is to become chief storytelling officer,

00:09:39.543 --> 00:09:42.913
always ready to tell the story
of the client or coworker

00:09:42.946 --> 00:09:45.349
or community member
whose life is made better

00:09:45.382 --> 00:09:47.150
by the work your team does.

00:09:47.985 --> 00:09:49.853
And if you're not in a leadership role,

00:09:49.886 --> 00:09:52.155
you can still motivate yourself
and other people

00:09:52.155 --> 00:09:54.491
by capturing every instance
that you come across,

00:09:54.524 --> 00:09:56.326
every time you hear about someone

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:58.395
who’s served by the work
that you’re doing.

00:09:58.395 --> 00:10:00.364
Every thank you that you get, capture it,

00:10:00.397 --> 00:10:04.501
save it for when you or anyone else
needs a powerful story

00:10:04.501 --> 00:10:07.638
about how the work
that we're doing matters.

00:10:08.672 --> 00:10:11.975
Because in the end, that's what
we all want from our work, isn't it?

00:10:11.975 --> 00:10:15.045
People want to do work that matters,

00:10:15.078 --> 00:10:18.348
and they want to work for leaders
who tell them they matter.

00:10:18.382 --> 00:10:21.118
And the most powerful way to tell them

00:10:21.118 --> 00:10:24.054
isn't to tell them some grandiose answer

00:10:24.087 --> 00:10:26.089
to “Why do we do what we do?”

00:10:26.123 --> 00:10:28.492
In fact, it isn't to tell them
anything at all.

00:10:28.525 --> 00:10:32.763
It's to help them find the answer
to the question "Who?"

00:10:33.797 --> 00:10:36.667
So let me ask you, do you know?

00:10:37.901 --> 00:10:41.204
Who is served by the work that you do?

00:10:43.206 --> 00:10:44.374
Thank you.

00:10:44.408 --> 00:10:46.610
(Applause)

